 3. 💥 MAHA&#x27;s White House revolt 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Calley Means speaks at a rally in Clive, Iowa, last month. Photo: Cody Scanlan/The Des Moines Register via Reuters Calley Means quickly rose from wellness entrepreneur to senior Trump health adviser — and now he's attacking the White House and suggesting top aides undermined the president's health agenda, Axios' Caitlin Owens and Adriel Bettelheim write. 

 Why it matters: His scorched-earth campaign — which continued yesterday in comments to Axios — marks a stunning turn for one of the administration's most vocal advocates of the "Make America Healthy Again" agenda. Means, who is widely credited with brokering the alliance between Robert F. Kennedy Jr. and President Trump in 2024, resigned from his administration post late last month.

 He told Axios he's going public with allegations of corruption — particularly involving former White House Chief of Staff Susie Wiles — because "it is existential to the survival [of] America that we rid DC of Susie's brand of politics." Means launched his attacks Friday on " The Joe Rogan Experience " and continued them Sunday on CBS' " Face the Nation " and across social media . 🖼️ The big picture: MAHA leaders have complained for months about a lack of progress on issues around healthy foods and pesticide use. 

 The intrigue: The MAHA base sees new chief of staff Stephen Miller as an ally. Miller's wife, Katie, hosted Health Secretary Robert F. Kennedy Jr. on her podcast and has posted photos of dishes with ingredients like raw milk.

