 5. 🏛️ House GOP&#x27;s power struggle 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 From left: Rep. Jim Jordan (R-Ohio) and Rep. Steve Scalise (R-La.) talk during a news conference at the Capitol in June. Photo: J. Scott Applewhite/AP House Republicans will choose a new leader this morning after last week's devastating election losses, Axios' Kate Santaliz writes. 

 Why it matters: Majority Leader Steve Scalise (La.) and Judiciary Chair Jim Jordan (Ohio) will headline the battle for control of what's left of the Republican conference — with every other top position also up for grabs. Scalise is pitching himself as the steady hand who can lead Republicans back to the majority, leaning on his decade of experience in leadership.

 Jordan is casting himself as the candidate for change, tapping into frustration among Republicans who believe last week's devastating losses demand a shake-up at the top . 👂 What we're hearing: Scalise allies are increasingly confident heading into today's vote, with Rep. Mario Diaz-Balart (R-Fla.) arguing that calls to delay the leadership elections are a sign that Jordan's supporters don't have the votes.

