 7. 🗳️ California voters reject billionaire tax 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sen. Bernie Sanders (I-Vt.) speaks at a rally in support of California's Proposition 40 in L.A. last month. Photo: Mike Blake/Reuters California voters rejected a first-in-the-nation tax on the net worth of billionaires, following a contentious ballot fight that generated huge spending by some prominent tech leaders.

 The defeat of the wealth tax is a win for Gov. Gavin Newsom and Gov.-elect Xavier Becerra, both Democrats, who opposed it . Critics said the tax would have pushed ultrawealthy taxpayers out of the Golden State. Why it matters: Proposition 40 was the nation's biggest test yet of an idea progressives have long championed — raising taxes on the ultra-rich by targeting their investments, AP reports . 

 The count is 53% to 47%, with 83% of votes in.

 AP called the race last night. Most Californians vote by mail. Any ballot postmarked by Election Day that arrives within a week is counted, meaning it often takes days or even weeks for close races to be settled. 💰 The "no" side raised roughly 10 times as much as the "yes" side. Google co-founder Sergey Brin alone dumped more than $100 million into a political action committee working to defeat the measure.

 The measure would have imposed a one-time 5% tax on the wealth of Californians with a net worth over $1 billion. Keep reading . 

 
 
